{
  // Nearest-tsconfig for the NODE serverless functions in this folder
  // (bot-page, share-meta, battle, health). Vercel's function builder compiles
  // each entrypoint with the closest tsconfig.json, so this decides the module
  // format their emitted .js is written in.
  //
  // It MUST emit CommonJS. The repo root declares no `"type"`, so Node reads
  // every .js under /var/task as CommonJS; an ESM emit dies on its first
  // `export`, before any handler code runs. That is not hypothetical — this file
  // said `"module": "ESNext"` from 2026-07-14 to 2026-07-27, and every Node
  // function in api/ returned FUNCTION_INVOCATION_FAILED on every route for the
  // whole of it:
  //
  //     Failed to load the ES module: /var/task/api/health.js.
  //     Make sure to set "type": "module" in the nearest package.json
  //
  // Nothing went red, because when the function is down the user-agent rewrites
  // in vercel.json do not fire and a crawler falls through to the SPA shell with
  // a healthy 200. `yarn smoke` now asserts it got the RIGHT page.
  //
  // The ESNext setting existed for one file: api/og.tsx needed `import.meta`.
  // That file now lives at api/og/index.tsx (same route) with its own tsconfig,
  // so each half gets the module format it actually needs.
  //
  // Two things not to do here:
  //   * Do not add `"type": "module"` in an api/package.json instead. Tried
  //     twice; both builds failed outright, and the bundler ships only files
  //     something imports, so a package.json never reaches /var/task/api/.
  //   * Do not raise `module` back to an ES target for a new file that wants
  //     `import.meta`. Give that file its own directory, the way api/og has.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "target": "ESNext",
    "lib": ["ESNext", "DOM"],
    "module": "CommonJS",
    "moduleResolution": "Node10",
    "ignoreDeprecations": "6.0",
    "types": ["node"],
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  }
}
